// Title: Suspicious WSL InstallLocation Registry Key Modification Via CommandLine
// Author: Swachchhanda Shrawan Poudel (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2026-05-05
// Level: high
// Description: Detects the use of reg.exe or PowerShell to modify the WSL InstallLocation registry key via command-line arguments.
// Legitimate modifications to this key are performed exclusively by the Windows Installer (msiexec.exe) during WSL package installation or update.
// Manual use of reg.exe or PowerShell to set this value strongly indicates an attempt to redirect WSL execution to a malicious binary.
// MITRE Tactic: Persistence
// Tags: attack.stealth, attack.defense-impairment, attack.persistence, attack.t1112, attack.t1218
// Reference: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/master/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_wsl_installlocation_modification_via_cmdline.yml
// False Positives:
//   - Unlikely

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (ProcessCommandLine contains " add " or ProcessCommandLine contains "New-ItemProperty" or ProcessCommandLine contains "Set-ItemProperty" or ProcessCommandLine contains "sp ") and (ProcessCommandLine contains "\\Lxss\\MSI" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/Lxss/MSI") and ProcessCommandLine contains "InstallLocation" and ((FolderPath endswith "\\powershell.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\pwsh.exe" or FolderPath endswith "\\reg.exe") or (ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName in~ ("powershell.exe", "pwsh.dll", "reg.exe")))